  "question": {
    "text": "In potassium ferrocyanide, there are ______ pairs of electrons in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow></msub></math> set of orbitals."
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "3",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Potassium ferrocyanide, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>CN</mi></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>, has a central iron ion that is coordinated with six cyanide&nbsp;ligands in an octahedral geometry. The oxidation state of the iron ion in potassium ferrocyanide is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math>,&nbsp;hence has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> hybridised state. On applying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math>. it shows that all the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math> electrons of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> subshell are present in the form of three pairs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow></msub></math> orbitals. Hence answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>.</p><p><img height=\"430\" src=\"assets\\st0118_b772449deafc.png\" width=\"582\" /></p></blockquote>"
  }
}